/* App-wide styling. Colors come from theme variables (see theme.py) so a
   theme swap recolors everything — no hardcoded hex here. */

/* The app runs in ANSI mode (ExpenseApp passes ansi_color=True), so the base
   fill is the terminal's OWN background — no seam against the terminal's window
   padding, on any terminal. Since 2026-08-19 the *foreground* is the terminal's
   too: the old `Screen:ansi { color: $foreground }` pin is gone, so text takes
   the terminal's default fg and the app reads on light grounds as well as dark.
   See docs/decisions.md "The terminal supplies the palette".

   NOTE the literal `ansi_default` here and on #modal below: `$surface`/`$panel`
   *generate* to `transparent` under an ANSI theme, so a token would make these
   see-through rather than terminal-coloured — the opposite of what a base fill
   and a modal card need. Structural rules (header rule, list-panel border) use
   `$secondary` for the same reason: they were drawn with `$surface` and would
   now be invisible. The modal scrim is the one deliberate literal — see the
   long note above ModalScreen:ansi. */
Screen {
    background: ansi_default;
}

/* ---- home ---- */
#brand {
    padding: 0 2;  /* single physical row — the wordmark+stats content is one line (see _build_header) */
    color: $foreground;
    text-style: bold;
    border-bottom: solid $secondary;  /* thin rule dividing header from menu */
}
/* No box: the menu sits directly on the terminal-transparent background (one
   surface with the screen, not a raised panel). It carries `ansi_default`
   itself rather than a token, for the reason given at the top of this file —
   a hardcoded fill here would relocate the seam to the menu edge. `border: none` overrides
   OptionList's default `tall` border; group headers, the inter-group
   separators, and the selection bar carry the structure the box used to. */
#menu {
    height: 1fr;
    margin: 1 1 1 1;
    background: ansi_default;
    border: none;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* ---- section interface (Outstanding Amounts) ---- */
#crumb {
    padding: 1 2;
    color: $text-muted;
    border-bottom: solid $secondary;
}
#content {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 2;
}
/* the section content card; its max-width is set per-screen (SectionScreen.
   CARD_WIDTH) so amounts sit in a tidy column rather than spreading wide. */
#card {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
}
.legend {
    color: $text-muted;
    margin-top: 1;
}
.sync-note {
    color: $text-muted;
    padding: 1 2;
}

/* quick-add log bar */
#inputbar {
    height: auto;
    margin: 1 2 0 2;
    max-width: 80;
    border: round $accent;
    padding: 0 1;
}
#field {
    width: 14;  /* longest label (BEGIN BALANCE, 13) + 1 right padding — guarded by test_tui_field_width.py */
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
    content-align: left middle;
    padding: 0 1 0 0;
}
#inputbar Input {
    border: none;
    background: transparent;
    width: 1fr;
}
#hint {
    color: $text-muted;
    padding: 0 3;
}
/* the LOG bar's legend — the grammar in one fixed row (pick A, 2026-08-29).
   Same gutter as #hint, directly under it. */
#legend {
    color: $text-muted;
    padding: 0 3;
}
#suggest {
    padding: 1 3 0 3;
    height: auto;
}
#summary {
    padding: 1 3;
    height: auto;
}

/* the LOG bar's staged list — same gutter as the form's summary it replaces */
#staged {
    padding: 1 3;
    height: auto;
}
/* the LOG bar's account peek: the ledger of the account the line names.
   `1fr` is the whole of mockup pick A (2026-08-29) — every widget above it is
   `height: auto`, so the panel is handed exactly the rows the staged list is
   not using, and AccountPeek draws that many. Quiet-border panel, same
   treatment as CursorList. */
#peek {
    height: 1fr;
    min-height: 0;
    margin: 0 3 1 3;
    border: round $secondary;
    border-title-color: $accent;
    border-title-style: bold;
    border-subtitle-color: $text-muted;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* modal screens — center the #modal box (else it collapses top-left) */
RecordModal, SnapshotModal, ConfirmModal, PromptModal, HelpModal, DiscardStagedModal {
    align: center middle;
}
/* The dim scrim (pick H, 2026-08-19). `black`, NOT `$background`, and that is
   the whole subtlety — learned by rendering it, not by reading it:

   `$background` is `ansi_default` under our theme, and alpha is DROPPED on an
   ANSI colour (`Color.parse("ansi_default").with_alpha(.6)` loses the ansi
   marker). So `background: $background 60%` resolves to an *opaque* fill and
   **wipes** the screen behind rather than dimming it — you lose sight of the
   very row you are confirming a delete on. Textual's own
   `ModalScreen:ansi { background: transparent }` can't save us either: our
   `Screen { background: ansi_default }` above out-specifies it.

   A literal `black 60%` composites properly over the terminal's ground, which
   is pick H exactly as its mockup legend stated it: the scrim is the one thing
   in the app not derived from the terminal, and on a light terminal it washes
   toward black rather than toward the ground. That cost was on the page when
   the pick was made. The card itself is opaque — see #modal below. */
ModalScreen:ansi {
    background: black 60%;
}
/* width is DEFINITE, not `auto`: inside `align: center middle` on Textual 8.x,
   `width: auto` measures to 0, collapsing the box to an empty border (Enter's
   detail view, the archive/delete confirm, and activity before/after all went
   invisible). max-width keeps it inside narrow terminals. Guarded by
   tests/unit/test_tui_modals.py so a Textual bump can't silently re-break it. */
#modal {
    width: 72;
    max-width: 90%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 80%;
    border: round $accent;
    background: ansi_default;
    padding: 1 2;
}
/* confirm/prompt carry short messages — a narrower box reads better */
ConfirmModal #modal, PromptModal #modal {
    width: 52;
    max-width: 90%;
}
.modal-title {
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
    margin-bottom: 1;
}
.section-title {
    color: $foreground;
    text-style: bold;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}
.sect {
    color: $accent;
    text-style: bold;
    margin: 1 0 0 0;
}
.error {
    color: $error;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* ---- Overview: the stock band (Accounts | People) ----
   Two 1fr halves above the four-month grid (mockup
   expense-world-reports-merge.html §12, picked 2026-08-29).

   `height: auto` on all three is load-bearing, not tidiness: #card is
   `height: auto` and Textual's Horizontal/Vertical default to `1fr`, so a
   1fr child inside an auto-height parent collapses to nothing — the same
   trap as the `width: auto` note further down this file.

   The 4-column gutter is not cosmetic either. It fixes an observed defect:
   without it both halves are 1fr and both Rich tables `expand`, so the left
   panel's right-aligned amount butts straight into the right panel's names
   and the row renders as `5,018.93Majo`. */
#band {
    height: auto;
}
#band-left {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    padding-right: 4;
}
#band-right {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
}

/* ---- list panels ----
   Every CursorList/CheckList is its own quiet-border panel (dossier option L2
   + treatment A, picked 2026-07-11): dim round border, accent title (absorbs
   the old title/legend line), muted subtitle carrying the page status
   (pagination pick B: `rows 21-40 of 133 · page 2 of 7`). Theme tokens only. */
CursorList, CheckList {
    border: round $secondary;
    border-title-color: $accent;
    border-title-style: bold;
    border-subtitle-color: $text-muted;
    padding: 0 1;
}
